+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf depth estimators; may lose high-frequency appearance details if primitives are downsampled too aggressively (mitigated by IBR-based color prediction) [S2].
+*   **When to use:** Large-scale urban scene reconstruction from sparse views where real-time rendering and global consistency are prioritized over per-scene fine-tuning [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with dense multi-view overlap where iterative optimization might yield higher fidelity; scenes where high-frequency texture details are critical and cannot be recovered via 2D context retrieval [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically as implemented in EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, et al. in 2025 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods that suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content due to per-pixel prediction. By predicting 3D Gaussians across multiple frames within a unified volume using a 3D convolutional network, it improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, exemplified by methods like EVolSplat and GRM, emerged from the need to accelerate 3D Gaussian Splatting from iterative optimization to feed-forward prediction. Traditional 3DGS requires slow, per-scene optimization, which is impractical for large-scale urban scenes or real-time applications [S2]. Early feed-forward methods like MVSplat and Flash3D predicted per-pixel Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, but these suffered from multi-view inconsistencies and ghosting artifacts when accumulated into a global scene [S2]. EVolSplat, introduced in 2025 by Miao et al., addressed these issues by predicting a global scene representation using a 3D convolutional network, refining noisy depth estimates through iterative offset updates, and leveraging 2D context for color prediction [S2]. GRM similarly utilizes existing multi-view generation models for faithful 3D generation via a single feed-forward pass [S1].
 
